"Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier French AI startup Mistral has raised a €3 billion Series D funding round led by Samsung Electronics and others, marking the largest funding round in European tech history. Secured a €3 billion Series D investment at a post-money valuation exceeding €21 billion just three years after founding, setting a record for the largest funding round in European tech history. Samsung Electronics led the round, with the Scaleup Europe Fund managed by EQT and existing investor PSG Equity participating as co-leads. The raised capital will be used to expand frontier research, scale compute capacity for model training, expand infrastructure, and accelerate global commercial deployment. Focuses on building a full-stack 'Sovereign AI' that delivers data sovereignty and independence from vendor lock-in, based on open-weight models and proprietary infrastructure. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral opens a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest economy—and accelerates collaboration in manufacturing and industrial AI as well as Physics AI. Mistral establishes a Munich hub to research industrial and physics AI, leveraging Germany's robust industrial data and expertise across automotive, energy, and aerospace sectors. To secure European AI sovereignty, the company aims to build 1GW of European compute capacity by 2030 and provide an open-weight architecture capable of running on customers' own infrastructure. Leveraging the physics and engineering AI capabilities of Emmi AI, acquired in May 2026, Mistral is pursuing partnerships with major manufacturing enterprises such as BMW (crash simulations) and Siemens Energy. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla have partnered to integrate privacy-enhanced Mistral open AI models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Smart Window'. Mozilla's Firefox Smart Window (beta) is powered by Mistral models, supporting tasks such as organizing complex searches and retrieving information across tabs. It will initially be available to users in France and North America, with expansion to the UK and Germany expected later this year. Through fine-tuning tailored to regional languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, it reflects local nuances while protecting privacy with non-storage of conversations and zero data retention. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera collaborate to bring specialized sovereign AI intelligence to enterprise data. Mistral's models are integrated into the Cloudera hybrid data platform to support direct inference execution across on-premises, public and private clouds, and air-gapped environments. It enables enterprises to build customized AI models using large-scale proprietary data within controlled environments while retaining ownership of both data and intelligence. It addresses the demand for sovereign AI, where the entire lifecycle of data, compute, model training, and inference can be controlled within customers' own infrastructure and jurisdiction. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Describes a case study by Mistral AI on successfully migrating a 40,000-line legacy Fortran 77 reservoir simulator into a modern C++ system using AI agents and a structured workflow. Converted 40,000 lines of physics-based Fortran 77 code from a European energy operator—which lacked test code and documentation—into object-oriented C++. Implemented a structured workflow combining AI agent-driven documentation and human review, while establishing a numerical consistency verification harness beyond simple syntax translation. Overcame constraints of the legacy language such as COMMON blocks and implicit type declarations, and carried out an architectural refactoring to enable integration with modern scientific computing frameworks like PETSc. Aleph Alpha Releases Kolibri: A 78.1B Open-Weight English-German MoE Model With Only 3.46B Active Parameters Germany's Aleph Alpha has released 'Kolibri,' a highly efficient 78.1B open-weight English-German MoE model that activates only 3.46B parameters per token. Featuring a MoE architecture that activates only 3.46B parameters—4.4% of its total 78.1B parameters—per token, it supports a context length of up to 1,048,576 tokens. Designed to comply with EU AI Act and GDPR regulations, it can run on a single B200, B300, or H200 GPU, or on two H100 SXM5 GPUs. Adopting sparse expert routing, hybrid attention, and a German-optimized UniBPE tokenizer, it reduces token count by 11.2% when processing German web text. DeepSeek Harness v0.2 Brings Official Desktop Apps to Its Open-Source Agent Harness DeepSeek has released the v0.2 preview of DeepSeek Harness (dsh), an open-source agent runtime, introducing official desktop apps and a plugin ecosystem. Official desktop apps for macOS (Apple Silicon) and 64-bit Windows have been released, enabling immediate execution without requiring separate installations of Node.js or pnpm. Featuring a Cordis-based architecture, the model adapter, tool registry, and agent loop are all designed as plugins, supporting third-party models and custom endpoints. Four session modes (Standard, Creator, PTC, Minimal) for general tasks and coding have been introduced, along with an experimental compatibility layer for Claude Code Mods. Ask GN: Which coding agent are you using? I'm curious about the reasons for your choice A community discussion post asking about real-world user experiences and reasons for choosing coding agents other than Claude Code from a non-developer's perspective. The author currently uses Claude Code but is considering adopting other tools as various coding agents emerge. As a user who does not write code directly, the author highlighted the ability to follow instructions and the prevention of unintended file modifications as key criteria. The author requested that real users share their experiences, including reasons for choosing their current agents, limitations or drawbacks encountered, and whether they rely on a single tool. LeCun Has \"Zero Concern\" Over AI Human Extinction and Recent 'Rogue' Incidents Yann LeCun dismissed concerns over the existential threat of AI and recent agent rogue incidents, emphasizing that improving system design and preventing regulatory capture are the core challenges. Recent rogue incidents involving AI agents stem from a lack of human oversight, such as poor control sandboxes and inadequate cybersecurity design, and are technically entirely preventable. He strongly criticized the Effective Altruism (EA) movement and AI executives' extinction warnings for stoking unnecessary public fear and negatively impacting the overall AI industry. He views 'regulatory capture'—where excessive regulations entrench the monopolies of large corporations and block entry for smaller competitors and open source—as a much greater real threat than the human extinction hypothesis. Claude Code Skill Patterns — How to Design Repetitive Tasks into Reusable Skills and Lessons Learned from Real-World Practice An article introducing the principles of effectively designing Claude Code skills and the practical experience gained from integrating skills and hooks into real-world development workflows. A skill's description serves as an agent invocation trigger rather than a simple explanation, so it must clearly state 'when to use it'. Streamline the skill body to focus on core procedures, completion criteria, and prohibitions, applying progressive disclosure by separating detailed materials into subdirectories. Separate pipeline skills from tool skills, and enforce mandatory rules or procedures through PreToolUse hooks rather than relying on skill instructions. What Agents Need Is Documentation, Not Memory The argument that to improve AI agents' understanding of project context and overall performance, what is needed is a structured documentation workspace and a continuous documentation update workflow, rather than RAG-based memory that searches fragments of past conversations. Conventional RAG memory—storing fragments of past conversations in vector databases and injecting retrieved snippets—fails to resolve issues with accuracy, recency, and lost context Going beyond a single AGENTS.md file, a structured documentation workspace containing guidelines, specifications, architectural decisions, and research findings is essential The workflow should transition to 'Prompt → Reference → Implement → Update' so agents keep documentation up to date right after completing tasks while the full context is still fresh Show GN: AutoroShopping - E-commerce Purchase Management Connector for AI Agents Introducing AutoroShopping, a purchase management connector enabling AI agents to automatically handle processes from product search to the pre-checkout stage on domestic e-commerce platforms Supports product search, adding to cart, pre-checkout automation, and tracking purchase history and price changes on major domestic shopping malls such as Coupang, Market Kurly, and Kyobo Book Centre. Provides a standardized purchasing workflow as a guardrail mechanism to ensure AI models safely execute tasks in accordance with procedures. Can be integrated with Claude Desktop, ChatGPT Desktop, and personal AI agent-dedicated clients, making it effective for handling recurring repurchases or multi-channel purchase requests. Top ARC-AGI-3 scores on Kaggle just went from 7% to 56% [N] Top scores on the ARC-AGI-3 benchmark in a Kaggle competition surged from 7% to 56% in just 30 days using small local models, surpassing average human levels. Despite Kaggle's environment constraints allowing only small local models, applying a harness led to a dramatic increase in top ARC-AGI-3 scores from 7% to 56%. This leap in scores took place over the past 30 days, beginning to surpass average human performance on a benchmark intentionally designed to demonstrate human superiority. Along with a leaderboard screenshot, the author seeks community thoughts on the rapid advancement of inference frameworks powered by small models. the official ICLR template .bib has had Bengio listed twice since 2019 [D] A report pointing out that Professor Bengio's name has been listed twice in the official ICLR paper template's bibliography file since 2019, along with contradictory page length guidelines. In the sample .bib file of the ICLR paper template, the authors of the Deep Learning textbook are duplicated as 'Goodfellow, Bengio, Courville, Bengio', and a non-existent volume 1 is included. A check on GitHub revealed that this error has persisted since the 2019 template to the present day. The author instructions contradictorily specify a main text limit of up to 9 pages in the submission section and 10 pages in the camera-ready and FAQ sections, so adhering to 9 pages is recommended. Working with an AI Company That Does Things You Disagree With [D] A request for advice from a machine learning PhD student debating whether to accept an internship offer from an AI company whose marketing strategy and products conflict with their ethical standards and values. An EU-based machine learning PhD student is conflicted over an internship offer from an AI company where the research team shows strong performance, but its marketing practices and product quality clash with the student's ethical values. Although the company relies on fear-based marketing, the research team's work and the mentoring environment themselves remain appealing. The author asks the community whether they should compromise for the sake of research experience, while also seeking 3-4 month internship opportunities at other organizations. A Minimal Interpretable Architecture for Zero-Shot Reconstruction of Dynamical Systems [R] This study proposes 'DynaBase', a minimal interpretable model that reconstructs the long-term statistical and geometric properties of dynamical systems using only a single parameter and a context selection mechanism. DynaBase consists solely of two components: a piecewise affine map with a single parameter α that controls local convergence/divergence rates, and a mechanism that selects context data closest to the current state. It accurately preserves key behavioral regimes of dynamical systems such as fixed points (α<1), limit cycles (α=1), and chaotic attractors (α>1), demonstrating long- and short-term forecasting performance that outperforms existing time-series and dynamical systems foundation models even in zero-shot settings. It enables low-cost training via an analytical single-step solution through linear regression or a single-parameter grid search, providing a mathematical framework for analyzing and improving the inner workings of foundation models. Nonobench: an open benchmark of 49 LLMs on nonogram puzzles, public and open source [P] Nonobench, an open-source benchmark evaluating the logical reasoning capabilities of 49 LLMs on nonogram (Picross) puzzles in a single attempt without tools, has been released. Evaluations were conducted using a Standard mode consisting of 30 puzzles ranging from 5x5 to 15x15 in size, and an Advanced mode consisting of 10 20x20 puzzles that cannot be solved using simple line logic alone. As the puzzle size increased, the average solve rate across all models dropped sharply from 85% (5x5) to 46% (10x10) and 20% (15x15). GPT-6 Astra solved all 30 puzzles in Standard mode; in Advanced mode, Claude Opus 5.5 solved 8 out of 10 puzzles, whereas 11 out of 15 models failed to solve a single puzzle. Pizza Bot: Open-Source Inbox for Background AI Agents This article covers Pizza Bot, an open-source, self-hosted tool released by AWS developers that allows users to manage background AI agent tasks using an email inbox-style interface. Pizza Bot is an Apache 2.0-licensed tool designed to manage long-running background AI agent workflows through an email inbox-style interface. It supports multiple model providers, Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers, and agent skills, adopting a local-first architecture where agent state, checkpoints, and logs are stored locally. It features scheduled executions or webhook-triggered workflows, subtask delegation to specialized agents, and human-in-the-loop interrupts to request approval before critical decisions. China-Aligned TA419 Targets U.S. AI Policy Experts With Microsoft AitM Phishing China-aligned cyber espionage group TA419 is launching phishing attacks targeting AI policy experts and research institutions in the United States. China-aligned hacking group TA419 carried out phishing attacks to steal account credentials of artificial intelligence (AI) experts across U.S. think tanks, universities, and legal sectors. The attackers impersonated prominent economists, AI policymakers, and Anthropic employees to target specific AI policy experts. Because the article text was truncated mid-sentence, further details regarding the attack methodology cannot be confirmed. Playing Games Without Looking at the Screen... GPT-6 Astra Clears 'WoW' in 40 Minutes OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra autonomously completed the starting quests in 'World of Warcraft' in just 40 minutes, relying solely on network packets and database analysis without visual screen input or direct manipulation. Instead of using visual data (computer vision) or keyboard and mouse controls, it analyzed server network packets and AzerothCore SQL data to independently build its own interface and action modules. Starting with a Level 1 Orc character, it completed all quests in the Valley of Trials in 40 minutes without dying once, demonstrating optimized pathing and resource management. It demonstrated the AI's long-term planning and reasoning capabilities by developing a C++ program integrated with the Detour pathfinding library to calculate routes and even leverage terrain bugs. \"Claude Works, Claude Watches\"... Anthropic Unveils New Plugin Anthropic has released a secondary agent plugin for Claude Code that monitors the main agent's workflow and summarizes key information for users. Anthropic added a built-in plugin called 'You should know' to Claude Code, which observes the main agent's work and displays important details in separate notes above the prompt. Within the developer community, praise for its practicality in assisting task tracking and review coexists with concerns over increased token consumption and cost inefficiency due to background surveillance. For security and user control, the feature is disabled by default and requires users to explicitly activate it using the command (/plugin enable cc-plugin-you-should-know@builtin). Aleph Alpha Unveils First Model 'Colibri' After Pivoting Away from General-Purpose LLMs... \"For Sovereign AI\" Germany's Aleph Alpha has unveiled 'Colibri', an open-weight model targeting data sovereignty in the defense and public sectors, following its decision to pivot away from general-purpose LLM development. A strategic model focused on data sovereignty (Sovereign AI) and EU regulatory compliance for the defense and public sectors, rather than competing with Big Tech in general-purpose performance. Designed with a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture activating only 3.46 billion parameters per token out of 78.1 billion total parameters, enabling on-premise deployment and high-efficiency operations. With 21.3% of pretraining tokens consisting of German text, it enhances processing of German administrative and legal contexts and incorporates an answer-abstention mechanism to reduce hallucinations. \"Four Instances of ARTEX, AI Hacking Tool Suspected in Shinhan Attack, Discovered in South Korea\" Traces of ARTEX, an open-source AI autonomous penetration testing tool, were detected in attack infrastructure targeting major domestic financial institutions, and analysis results of exposed infrastructure globally and in South Korea were revealed. Strings related to the LLM-based open-source penetration testing tool ARTEX were discovered in the web server title suspected of being used in the attack on Shinhan Bank, though official confirmation of its use remains unverified. According to analysis by Oasis Security's CTI platform, 359 ARTEX infrastructures were identified based on unique IPs, concentrated in the US (65.7%), China (14.8%), and Hong Kong (10.9%), with 4 also discovered in South Korea. While ARTEX is originally a security validation tool, attackers can exploit it to automate reconnaissance and vulnerability discovery, and it was found to be heavily concentrated and operated across specific hosting networks such as PEG TECH INC. TNaps Named Top 10 in 'NVIDIA Inception Challenge 2026' Just Over a Year After Inception AI security startup TNaps has been selected as one of the Top 10 in the Asia-Pacific region at the 'NVIDIA Inception Grand Challenge 2026', recognized for its technological prowess in real-time verification and control of AI agent decision-making. Just over a year after its founding, TNaps was named in the final Top 10 at the APAC finals of the 'NVIDIA Inception Grand Challenge 2026', which featured around 10,000 participating companies. The company presented a solution that places a 'Decision Trust Infrastructure' verification layer between an AI agent's judgment and execution, controlling whether actions are executed based on enterprise policies and risk levels. It applied real-time runtime optimization based on NVIDIA GPUs and TensorRT-LLM, and is pursuing expansion across diverse industries and global enterprises beyond the financial sector such as KB Kookmin Bank. U.S. Grocery Prices Surge 32% in 6 Years… Consumers Turn to AI to Ease the Burden of Grocery Shopping Amid soaring grocery prices in the United States, consumers are leveraging AI tools like ChatGPT to compare prices, find budget-friendly alternatives, and cut down on food expenses. With U.S. grocery prices rising 32% compared to early 2020 and wage growth lagging behind inflation, household grocery burdens are intensifying. Consumers are uploading photos of receipts and store shelves to ChatGPT to compare prices, choosing private-label (store brand) products, and relying on food assistance or debt to lower food expenses. Thanks to efforts to curb grocery expenses, spending in other sectors such as dining out has remained resilient, driving consumer spending growth to its fastest pace in over a year this past August. [AI Leaders] Jun Morita, Head of Perplexity APAC: \"Establishing a Korean Subsidiary Within the Year to Expand AI Choices\" U.S. AI company Perplexity plans to establish a Korean subsidiary within the year to strengthen partnerships with domestic enterprises and expand its multi-LLM-based AI agent ecosystem. Perplexity is preparing to establish a Korean subsidiary within this year, aiming to expand collaboration with domestic conglomerates, SMEs, and startups. Following its collaboration with SK Telecom and technology integration into Samsung Electronics' Galaxy S26, the company is in discussions for additional partnerships with multiple hardware manufacturers. Moving beyond reliance on a single model, Perplexity is actively driving 'Perplexity Computer,' a multi-model AI agent service that combines, executes, and verifies tasks across 20 to 30 LLMs. [AI Now] Chasing ChatGPT and Claude? Google Launches 'Gemini 4' in Full-Scale Tech, Price, and Marketing Offensive Google has unveiled its next-generation flagship model 'Gemini 4 Argon' and cut domestic subscription prices by 40%, launching an all-out offensive to expand its AI capabilities and market share. Google unveiled its next-generation frontier AI model 'Gemini 4 Argon', significantly expanding its maximum output tokens to 1 million. In the domestic market, Google discounted the annual subscription fee for 'Google AI Pro' by 40% to broaden its user base and launched a new celebrity-fronted advertising campaign. The Argon model achieved outstanding results on software engineering and professional task automation benchmarks, launching with competitive API pricing."